          "course": "Anglais",
          "passage": "Developing agriculture ans small industry in rural DRC, which is crucual for poverty reduction, depends heavily on reopening rural roads. In this context the sector has benefited from massive donor support.\n\nThe word Bank for instance is implementing a major project of over U.S § 50 million for the \"High- Priority Roads Reopening and Maintenance\", which development objective is to re-establish lasting access between provincial capitals and districts. The latter should re-open about 1,800 kilometres of the high-priority road networrk and ensure their maintenance thereafter. The land connection between the provinces of south kivu and katanga, and within Oriental province should be re-establish. This road network will serve the highest populated ares and connect a network of about 7,000 km of high-priority roads already funded by various donors.\n\nThe roads targeted are kisangani-Buta (at the bordeline with south sudan )-Bondo-Bunduki (620km) crossing Oriental province and connecting to Equateur province; and Uvira - kasomeno (1,180km) crossing the south lkivu at the frontier with Burundi Republic and katanga provinces.\n\nChina is also a major player in the sector, a,nd recently earmarked U.S § 3 billion of a U.S § 5 billion loan for transport development during 2007. Within the latter, a road project linking Kisangani in oriental province with Kasumbalesa in southern Katanga, a major frontier post with Zambia will be implemented.\n\nBelgium and the DFID (British development cooperation) are also intervening heavily with several projects. Rehabilitation of major roads should facilitate trade. these projects include: reparation of 1,900km of roads in Kongo Central, in the cataractes ans Bas-fleuve districts; 721 km of roads in Eastern Kasai, to open up the Kabinda/Lubao region, The Ngandajika region and the city of Mbuji-may; 131 km of roads between Mbuji-may and lusambo; 125 km of roads in Bandundu and 110 km in Kongo central to allow agricultural products to be transported to big cities.\n\nRecognizingthe vulnerability of earth roads to rainfalls and heavy trucks even if limited in num er, an exten,sive maintenance programme has also been put together, funded by a newly created Road Maintenance Fund.",
